useful applications in machine

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:13.270
learning.

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:15.910
So we're going to start
with some use cases.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.600
So I couldn't resist.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.030
I know that you had an astronaut
here today and I thought,

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.738
I had to take a photo
that you should all

00:00:22.738 --> 00:00:25.440
know from the Kuiper Belt.
So the little blue dot

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.000
there, that's us.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.210
In 1990, this was Voyager
turning back a six billion--

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.660
yeah, six billion kilometers
from Earth and actually

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.020
looking and saying bye.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.160
And that's all that was left
of us, this little blue dot.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.940
So we're going to zoom in a bit,
because my use case is actually

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.800
done here on Earth.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.570
This is my favorite
view of the Earth.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.776
Every time I get on in plane
and I fly across the Atlantic,

00:00:48.776 --> 00:00:50.400
I start playing around
with that screen

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.100
once I've watched all
20 films I didn't see.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.320
And then I start looking at
where the plane's flying.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.510
And then I'm twiddling
the globe around.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.400
And I always like to
get this backward face

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.770
of the globe, where you
can't see any land on it.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.306
I think it's a really nice
way of looking at the planet.

00:01:04.306 --> 00:01:05.930
And it gives you a
kind of perspective.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:08.610
It really kind of messes
with your head a little bit.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.890
You have to think, is it really?

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.500
So this use case I'm
going to tell us about--

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.200
actually, whenever
we tell this use case

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.300
at Google for a
conference, for example,

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.162
we always mention this island.

00:01:21.162 --> 00:01:22.620
And I didn't know
where it was so I

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.140
thought I'd take the effort
and find out where it was.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.820
And it happened to be slap
bang in the middle of that view

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.536
that I like to look at so much.

00:01:31.536 --> 00:01:33.660
And I don't know if you
can see this here properly.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.210
So the island is
called Kiribati.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.355
And this island
has villages on it.

00:01:38.355 --> 00:01:40.230
This is nothing to with
machine learning yet.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.540
This it just really interesting.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:49.680
So this island has villages on
it of London, Paris, Poland,

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:50.810
and Banana.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.780
So like, if you ever look
into anything, you can--

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.789
so, what is the use case?

00:01:56.789 --> 00:01:58.080
The use case is global fishing.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.280
Maybe some of you've
heard about it.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.650
It's a really cool use case.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:02.730
And normally we
show this picture.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.190
So we show this picture of
all these boat moving around,

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.054
and Africa and Madagascar.

00:02:07.054 --> 00:02:08.970
So I thought Kiribati
was there, but it's not.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.979
It's on the other side.

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:14.100
So that was my little
adventure into coming up

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.690
to this use case.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:16.820
So what's going on here?

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:20.370
Basically, whenever
a fishing boat

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.930
passes through a
certain zone, they

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:25.750
want to know if they're actually
allowed to be fishing there.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.830
And whenever they catch someone
that's illegally fishing,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.930
they say, oh, we were
just passing through.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.550
Yeah, that's a bit of a problem.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.950
OK, if their nets were down
in the water, but you know,

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:36.540
you've got some time
to get the nets out.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.289
The say, oh, we were
just passing through.

00:02:38.289 --> 00:02:40.590
So the machine learning
application that they looked at

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.298
was actually to take
this data, and we're

00:02:42.298 --> 00:02:44.310
talking about 40
billion rows of events,

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.120
and we've got 200,000 ships
broadcasting AIS all the time.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.250
That's the little
positioning stuff for boats.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.110
And what they did
was actually work out

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.180
how these boats are
traversing the water,

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.390
and what patterns correspond
to fishing patterns.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.420
And they applied this, and
so they could actually now

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.120
actually start finding
well, fishing companies

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.950
that were illegally fishing.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.210
And one of the first ever
successful prosecutions

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:12.570
was in the waters of Kiribati.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:15.480
And they fine that they actually
issued was $2.2 million,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.240
which was 1% of the
country's global GDP.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.450
This is something you can
do with machine learning.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.966
I'm guessing most people here
are interested in fishing

00:03:23.966 --> 00:03:25.090
waters and stuff like that.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:26.464
So this is like,
really take note

00:03:26.464 --> 00:03:28.300
and you can start
applying this as well.

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:33.746
I'm going to take a few use
cases today from retail.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:35.120
The reason I'm
going to do retail

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.480
is because I think
it's quite broad

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.460
and everyone has some
association with retail.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.340
Either we're working with
retail or we're involved

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.230
in buying for ourselves.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.870
So we've got a
good understanding.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:47.814
So Ocado is a
British supermarket.

00:03:47.814 --> 00:03:48.480
They are online.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.660
They're online only.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.590
It's got a ton of
active customers.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.840
Well five tons of
them, to be precise.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.361
And basically what
they want to do,

00:03:56.361 --> 00:03:58.110
they want to process
all the messages that

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:58.960
are coming at them.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.360
So they've got all
these messages coming in

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.654
on email and social media
and things like that.

00:04:02.654 --> 00:04:05.070
And if one of their customers
is unhappy about something--

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.470
maybe the delivery didn't
turn up on time, or the food

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.050
was damaged, or whatever-- they
want to capture that message

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.250
and act.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.560
So they're-- you know,
customer satisfaction is really

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:14.740
important to them.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:17.190
So they have to process
all these messages.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.920
This message here is
like someone, I'm happy.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.579
You know, my children
love the website.

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:22.819
It's so easy, and blah, blah.

00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:23.360
That's great.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.136
We'll write back to
that, but maybe later.

00:04:25.136 --> 00:04:26.760
The thing that we
need to deal with now

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.634
is maybe another message
that's coming at us,

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.330
which is, where's my food?

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.950
So it's a way that
they can actually

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:35.340
take text, unstructured
text, and basically

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.950
process that, apply NLP, so
Natural Language Processing.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:40.422
We offer an API for this.

00:04:40.422 --> 00:04:41.880
And they can actually
then find out

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.250
what the sentiment of the
text is, extract the entities,

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:45.720
and they can then
go and use this

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:50.246
to actually help provide a
better customer satisfaction.

00:04:50.246 --> 00:04:52.620
This, and I'm not going to
read them all out because it's

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:53.703
going to take a long time.

00:04:53.703 --> 00:04:56.310
But these are some of
the areas within retail

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.620
where you might want to
look at machine learning.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.010
And there's actually
quite a lot.

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.310
I mean, it ranges from
everything from supply chain

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.480
to pricing to customer insights.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:08.514
So we've got this whole range,
so it's not just in one area.

00:05:08.514 --> 00:05:10.430
It might be that you're
looking for anomalies.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:12.140
It might be you're trying
to forecast a trend,

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:13.306
or you're trying to predict.

00:05:13.306 --> 00:05:15.087
You're trying to
make recommendations.

00:05:15.087 --> 00:05:16.670
So that all these
kind of applications

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:18.740
that you can use
machine learning for

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:22.250
to actually improve your
business, whether that's inside

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:23.810
to actually become
more economic,

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:25.820
or outside to improve
your relationships

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:28.402
with your customers, or
generate more revenue, all

00:05:28.402 --> 00:05:29.360
those important things.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:33.940
This is just a number.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.222
It's done by a research company.

00:05:36.222 --> 00:05:37.180
But I want this number.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:38.310
It's just to basically think.

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.310
This is not the only
important number out there.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.840
But nine in 10
people in this survey

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:48.480
said, if the company was able
to predict my intent better

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.240
and suggest more
intuitive products,

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.760
I would use them
above everyone else.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.610
Now I know there are tons
of research firms coming up

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.290
with tons of
statistics like this,

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.248
but I just want to think
about it for a moment.

00:06:00.248 --> 00:06:03.570
I just want to ask you to think
about, OK if this is happening,

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:07.770
if customers are starting to
expect something more from us,

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:11.360
can we afford not to do this?

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.870
I would say that
the answer is no.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.750
Maybe-- I mean, I'm sure
there's a lot of discussion

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:19.920
to be had around that.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.460
But if I look at my own
life, my personal life,

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.380
I see more and
more how I interact

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.260
with machine learning and AI.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:29.880
Or the result, the
product of that,

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.970
whether it's, for example,
when I connect to Spotify

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.280
and I actually want to
find some new music,

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.680
or when it's, as Bill
showed us earlier,

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.494
I'm trying to work on
a document like now,

00:06:39.494 --> 00:06:41.160
and I can just go and
find the document.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:42.993
It's becoming more and
more something I use.

00:06:42.993 --> 00:06:44.940
And it's becoming more
and more intuitive.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.760
And I think our
customers, they really

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.990
appreciate advice which
actually seems-- this is not

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:53.310
something that is obstructive.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.950
This is something that is
just actually improving

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:58.540
my engagement with you.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.310
So I think that companies are
already acting in this space.

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.920
Companies are actively
thinking about this space.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.670
They have a great
chance of actually going

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:07.850
forward and being
more successful

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:08.770
with their customers.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:11.880
But what this means is that
there's a lot of work that

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.350
needs to be done
behind the scenes

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.120
to actually get that
information out so

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:16.680
we can start doing these things.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.139
Recommendation engines don't
just pop up out of the ground,

00:07:19.139 --> 00:07:19.638
you know?

00:07:19.638 --> 00:07:22.140
You have to think about, what
data am I going to use for it?

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:23.700
How am I going to
collect that data?

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:24.780
How am I going to process it?

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:26.010
Which model should I choose?

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.960
And when you've done all of
those things and you think,

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.070
well great, I have this
fantastic model now.

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:30.930
It's able to predict
stuff for me,

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.940
and my customers really like it.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:33.970
That was today.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:36.750
Tomorrow, there's more
insight, there's more data.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:37.920
Things are changing.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.810
You need to go and
retrain that model,

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.700
and see if your new
model is actually

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:45.330
more predictive and more useful
than what you had before.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:48.120
If you're not doing
that, then you're static.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.580
These things don't just
learn by themselves.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:55.320
You need to go and actually feed
them new data and retrain them.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:59.130
So this is a very simple naive
look at a neural network.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:01.890
Neural networks are immensely
popular at the moment

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:03.600
for all sorts of
things, including

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.730
things like recommendations.

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:08.670
There are more classical
regression models

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:12.644
and [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:12.644 --> 00:08:14.310
There are more classical
things that you

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:15.960
could use to do
a recommendation,

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.780
like collaborative
filtering for example.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:19.440
But people are starting to use--

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.630
they're looking at the
capacity of neural networks,

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.120
because neural networks have
something that's really quite

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:25.410
unique to them, I would say.

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:28.410
And that's the ability
to generalize very well.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:31.300
If you can't generalize,
what does that mean?

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.890
It means for example, if I
was creating a model which

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:35.940
is taking advantage of a
whole load of features,

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.980
and then trying to
get a good regression,

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:40.289
and let's say I'm overfitting
because I'm just plugging

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:40.950
all that stuff in there.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:42.720
And I think OK, this
is working well.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:46.830
But then I go and apply
that to a new set of people,

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.270
where they're in
the same domain,

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:51.420
but let's say, on
the edge of it.

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:52.440
And things are changing.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:54.390
My model, if it's
too specific, it

00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:57.811
might not really recognize their
behavior or what's going on.

00:08:57.811 --> 00:08:59.310
Deep neural networks
help with that.

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:00.870
They're very good
at generalizing.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.120
So for recommendations,
there's actually a couple ways

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:05.880
that you can take like
good parts of regression,

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.400
with a more, let's say,
a more classical set up.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:12.090
And then go for a deeper neural
network in the same model

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:16.170
to actually get
a model which has

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:19.250
both aspects of memorization,
so learning the things, very

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:20.610
important, they've seen before.

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:23.880
But at the same time, flexible
enough to actually be useful

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:27.000
and not just come with the
same answer because it's this.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.910
So what's changed in
the last few years?

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:35.120
Why is this all happening now?

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:38.620
Well, machine learning has
been around for a long time.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:41.815
And a lot of the models that
you see, especially used

00:09:41.815 --> 00:09:44.350
in competitions up
until a few years ago,

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:47.020
and still when people
are running this

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:48.520
by themselves in
their own machines,

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:51.880
are kind limited by the amount
of processing power you had.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:54.130
If you think about a
good model, a good model

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:56.590
implies that it's
probably going to have

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:58.520
to do quite a lot of work.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.850
And then on top of that,
we've got a lot of data.

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:02.590
Now if we mash those
two things together,

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:04.930
we need a lot of those
things on the right

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:06.400
to start processing it.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.575
These things on the
right here are our TPUs.

00:10:08.575 --> 00:10:09.900
And you don't have to use TPUs.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:11.400
You could use GPUs
or anything else.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:14.260
But TPUs are actually
very useful for us.

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:15.695
We brought our
second generation.

00:10:15.695 --> 00:10:17.320
This is worth standing
still, because I

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:18.986
think this is one
that differentiates us

00:10:18.986 --> 00:10:21.830
with Google Cloud in the
space of machine learning.

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:25.865
So these things are 180
teraflops per circuit.

00:10:25.865 --> 00:10:27.880
They're A6, so they're
designed specifically

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:30.190
for a certain task.

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:31.630
In this case
actually calculating

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:33.410
floating point operations.

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:35.210
180 tera, what does that mean?

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:37.240
It means, if we
put them in a pod,

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:39.780
and I'll show you a
photo that in a moment--

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:42.865
sorry, Shelby,
and she knows why.

00:10:42.865 --> 00:10:44.740
Well, Shelby said to
let you all in the joke.

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:46.365
When I showed her
this photo, she said,

00:10:46.365 --> 00:10:49.000
it looks like something
from the 1970s.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:50.725
I'm a kind of 1970s guy.

00:10:50.725 --> 00:10:51.604
But I like the '70s.

00:10:51.604 --> 00:10:53.020
I like a lot of
stuff in the '70s,

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:54.430
so this really appeals to me.

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:56.530
These are all pods of
actually of those TPUs.

00:10:56.530 --> 00:10:58.990
Each pod is 11
and 1/2 petaflops.

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:00.700
What do those numbers
mean in real terms?

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.200
What do they mean
for anyone else?

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:04.980
They mean, we were
translating-- for example,

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:07.120
we were taking our
models like Translate

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:09.220
and retraining that
in a couple of days.

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.720
And now we can do it
in a couple of hours.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:14.910
So if you think about these use
cases that maybe were involving

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:16.910
lots of data and
lots of complexity,

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:20.410
now we have the ability to
actually go and retrain them

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:22.270
in relatively short
amount of time,

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.570
and to get a new model out
there which could potentially

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:29.000
impact our conversation
with our users.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.990
So the message I want
to take from this

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:35.260
is that machine learning is
a really good fit with cloud.

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:37.819
Cloud offers you this,
it will provide you

00:11:37.819 --> 00:11:39.610
this infrastructure to
go and actually take

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:40.940
advantage of that.

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:42.250
So how do we do that?

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:43.440
Well, we do it in two ways.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.190
So on the one hand,
on the right hand side

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:47.330
here, we have our managed APIs.

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:50.530
This is a little bit about the
democratization of the machine

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:51.170
learning.

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:53.128
So what we're trying to
do in the machine space

00:11:53.128 --> 00:11:54.550
is get more people involved.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:58.083
I just explained in my last
talk that I have two children,

00:11:58.083 --> 00:12:00.416
and whilst I'm not sure if
my children are going to grow

00:12:00.416 --> 00:12:02.080
up and become data scientists--

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:05.410
I think one wants to be a
firefighter and the other one,

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:06.410
we're going to find out.

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:09.847
But I know when they grow up and
they participate in the world,

00:12:09.847 --> 00:12:11.430
machine learning is
going to be there.

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:12.490
It's already here now.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:14.980
We're on the cusp and things
are just accelerating.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:17.537
I want my kids to know
what machine learning is,

00:12:17.537 --> 00:12:19.120
what it's about, and
more importantly,

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:20.472
what people are using it for.

00:12:20.472 --> 00:12:22.180
I don't want it to be
something that they

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:23.929
don't have that
connection with the world,

00:12:23.929 --> 00:12:26.140
that the world just
kind of passes them by.

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:30.190
In our effort to democratize
artificial intelligence

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:32.530
and machine learning,
what we're trying to do

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:34.810
is provide a space for
people to interact with.

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:37.060
On the right hand side,
we're taking models

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:39.460
that we've actually trained
to solve, let's say,

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:40.570
common problems--

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:45.160
OCR, taking a translation,
going from speech to text.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.580
We're opening them
up on open APIs

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:50.300
that developers can use
in their applications,

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:53.480
making single rest calls
to actually get a result.

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:55.570
So you saw the video
demo earlier, you know,

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:57.050
those kind of things.

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:59.920
So that's one way of opening
up at least the usage

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:01.450
and getting more
people interacting

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:02.770
with the capabilities.

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:06.370
On the left hand side, we have
TensorFlow and Cloud Machine

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.470
Learning.

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:11.020
I would say the big
differentiator between Google

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:13.210
Cloud and other
clouds, and I think

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:15.430
that other clouds are
actually following us,

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:17.397
so we definitely have
the lead in this space.

00:13:17.397 --> 00:13:19.480
And it was the reason why
I joined Google as well.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:21.297
I'm a big data fan.

00:13:21.297 --> 00:13:23.630
And if you look at the big
data technology, a lot of it,

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:24.970
there's a relationship
with Google--

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:26.530
either they published
white papers,

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:29.440
or, in the most recent
years, open sourcing a ton

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:30.550
of frameworks.

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:32.410
So why is this important?

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:36.340
TensorFlow is an open sourced
project for machine learning.

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:39.580
It has something like
50,000 stars on GitHub.

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:41.980
So you saw Linux has 20,000.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:44.702
Imagine how many people are
involved with this then?

00:13:44.702 --> 00:13:45.910
We know everyone knows Linux.

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:47.740
People have an
affiliation with Linux.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:52.930
So 50,000, that's 1 and 1/2
times more, are actually

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:53.889
they're using--

00:13:53.889 --> 00:13:55.930
I should say 2 and 1/2
times-- but they're there,

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:58.821
using it, experimenting with it.

00:13:58.821 --> 00:14:00.820
So first of all, there's
a lot of collaboration.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:04.420
That innovation that we spoke
about earlier, about actually

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:06.040
having people
contribute to the space

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:08.710
and seeing what's going
on and moving the needle,

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:11.050
is the same thing what's
happening in machine learning.

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:13.508
Having it open source means we
can all collaborate and work

00:14:13.508 --> 00:14:14.380
together.

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:16.580
And we have a managed service.

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:20.075
So we do that for our big
data frameworks as well.

00:14:20.075 --> 00:14:22.450
But we have managed service
that you can take your model,

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:23.530
come and run at us.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:27.590
It kind of puts the kind of,
let's say responsibility,

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:29.790
square on our shoulders
to do a couple of things--

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.880
make sure that we're
giving you the best price,

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:34.430
make sure that we're
providing the best security,

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:36.500
make sure we're providing
the best performance.

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:38.700
And if not, you've
written it in open source,

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:40.530
so you can go and run
it somewhere else.

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:42.410
So you know your
investment's in good hands.

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:44.960
Also, if a lot of people
are working on this project,

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:47.450
it means there are a lot of
developers and data scientists

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:49.317
and analysts out there
in the market that

00:14:49.317 --> 00:14:50.150
are working with it.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:52.130
So when you're looking
to recruit more people

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:55.742
to actually accelerate
your effort in that space,

00:14:55.742 --> 00:14:57.200
there are people
in the market that

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.000
understand this technology.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:01.250
You don't have to go and
just start from scratch

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:02.900
and train them in something new.

00:15:02.900 --> 00:15:04.850
So open source is
a really big part

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:07.004
of machine learning as well.

00:15:07.004 --> 00:15:08.420
If you're not using
an open source

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:11.750
framework for your machine
learning, shame on you.

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:13.460
You really should
start considering it,

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:14.960
because it really will
put you in a better

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:15.960
position for the future.

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:20.709
This is a quick architecture.

00:15:20.709 --> 00:15:22.750
And we don't have to go
for all the details here,

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:24.860
but what I basically say,
this is the architecture

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:27.170
of the Video API.

00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:29.570
The most difficult part
of this, the part that

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:32.270
cost the most time to
program, was the front end,

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:33.414
the stuff that you saw.

00:15:33.414 --> 00:15:34.580
And we do that in AppEngine.

00:15:34.580 --> 00:15:36.740
[CHIRPING]

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:40.190
My egg is now hard boiled.

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:43.070
So we do that in AppEngine.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:45.410
All the stuff on the
bottom is basically

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:46.880
completely serverless.

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:50.450
So you take your video, you
upload it to Cloud Storage.

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:52.730
We have serverless functions
that are listening.

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:54.440
And if there's more
events happening,

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:57.110
we will spin up
automatically more functions

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:59.870
and then with each video we
pick up, we will call the API,

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:02.600
process it, extract
the metadata.

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:05.510
We take a metadata, so like,
the labels that you saw earlier,

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:06.890
which Lee demonstrated.

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:08.690
We see which index it happens.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:11.342
And we will serve that up, in
this case with putting it back

00:16:11.342 --> 00:16:12.050
on Cloud Storage.

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:13.130
But we could put
that in a database

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:14.588
or whatever else
we want to put it,

00:16:14.588 --> 00:16:17.570
so we can make it searchable and
available for our application.

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:21.000
So if you think, if you've got
something around those APIs

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:23.000
that we just mentioned,
its like speech to text,

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:26.690
translation, processing
videos, processing images,

00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:29.720
extracting sentiment from
images or recognizing where

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:30.970
people are standing on images.

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:32.390
There are many
things you can do.

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:34.181
And you can just use
a service architecture

00:16:34.181 --> 00:16:35.645
and really get up to--

00:16:35.645 --> 00:16:38.494
hit the ground running.

00:16:38.494 --> 00:16:40.160
If you're going to
go deeper, and you're

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:42.450
going to start building
some of the stuff yourself,

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:43.670
there are a few things
I want to leave you with

00:16:43.670 --> 00:16:45.355
to actually think
about in that process.

00:16:45.355 --> 00:16:46.730
Make sure the
developers that are

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:49.040
working for you are
giving you good answers

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:50.580
on these questions.

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:52.520
So we want to do
machine learning.

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:55.624
We want to be here at the top
doing our machine learning.

00:16:55.624 --> 00:16:57.540
We want to start getting
this predictive power

00:16:57.540 --> 00:16:59.590
making a business impact.

00:16:59.590 --> 00:17:02.600
It is right at the
top of this stack.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:04.760
Right at the bottom
is infrastructure.

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:06.920
So again, using a
framework like TensorFlow

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:09.980
that you can run on
basically any architecture,

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:11.910
that gives you that capability.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:14.030
If you're using
another project which

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:16.130
doesn't have those
capabilities, and then you

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:18.780
want to take something and
bring it into production,

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:21.710
you're going to have to have
your engineers find a way

00:17:21.710 --> 00:17:24.119
to actually take that
from the research space

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:26.030
into the production space.

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:30.020
That is one of the key
motivators behind TensorFlow

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:31.550
inside Google itself.

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:33.449
The Google Brain team,
they were working,

00:17:33.449 --> 00:17:34.740
they were doing research stuff.

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:36.882
And then when they tried
to move it to production,

00:17:36.882 --> 00:17:38.840
basically there had to
be an engineering effort

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:40.495
to take those learnings
and that model,

00:17:40.495 --> 00:17:42.870
and now how are we going to
get this thing in production?

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:45.200
So having infrastructure
that we can do that on,

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:48.020
and having the applications
that actually facilitate that,

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:50.090
is a very powerful thing.

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:52.804
As we move up, we
come to the kind of,

00:17:52.804 --> 00:17:54.470
let's say the body
of water and the data

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:56.090
that we need to
actually run this on.

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:57.950
Some of the key challenges
there are around

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:00.080
collecting data,
processing data.

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:01.640
These are all major
efforts that need

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:04.140
to be made if you're actually
going to use machine learning.

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:06.211
So you have to have good
answers to these things.

00:18:06.211 --> 00:18:08.210
And then on the top, we
can do machine learning.

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:12.369
If you don't do it like
this, you end up like this.

00:18:12.369 --> 00:18:13.910
And this is basically
what'll happen.

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:14.880
You'll be up there
and you'll be like,

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:16.421
oh, I've got something
in production.

00:18:16.421 --> 00:18:21.020
And bomb, it's gone, and
you've got a big problem.

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:22.692
This architecture,
and I won't go

00:18:22.692 --> 00:18:24.650
into too many details
about this at the moment,

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:26.810
but I will just say that
this is an architecture we

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:29.310
use to help customers
build things

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:30.560
when they're using TensorFlow.

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:32.840
And the important
part to look at here

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:34.700
is that we have
frameworks like data flow

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:41.080
which is this one here, which
is actually unified batch--

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:42.500
a streaming framework.

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:45.690
What that means is,
I can take an action

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:48.320
I want to perform on my data,
like mapping, or a function

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:50.480
that I want to perform,
a processing action,

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:52.534
and I can apply
it to batch data.

00:18:52.534 --> 00:18:54.200
So think about the
data that you already

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:55.970
have that you can use
to train your model.

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:59.090
But I can also apply it to
real time data, data coming in

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:00.050
on a stream.

00:19:00.050 --> 00:19:02.210
The same model, just
making a few changes

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:03.900
of how the data's
actually coming in.

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:05.940
That's what a unified
model allows you to do.

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:06.920
So if you look at
the bottom there,

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:07.640
we have date flow coming.

00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:09.890
So when you're ready to
make those predictions,

00:19:09.890 --> 00:19:11.120
sometimes people forget.

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:13.310
I've just spent all this
time taking this data,

00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:15.680
extracting all the features,
and then I trained a model.

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:16.980
Now we're going to
go make predictions.

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:18.520
What are you going to
make predictions on?

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:19.910
Yeah, I'm going to make
a prediction on this.

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:22.420
OK, so what do we have to do
with that to actually get it

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:24.050
in the same space
as our prediction?

00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:25.758
Yeah, you have to
extract those features,

00:19:25.758 --> 00:19:28.010
and now you see why
having a unified model

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:29.585
might actually make
a lot of sense.

00:19:29.585 --> 00:19:32.129
They're both serverless as
well, and again, as I said,

00:19:32.129 --> 00:19:33.420
they have open source projects.

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:35.503
Apache VM, which you can
create your stuff online.

00:19:35.503 --> 00:19:36.712
You develop this crazy stuff.

00:19:36.712 --> 00:19:39.086
All the things I said about
TensorFlow apply to Apache VM

00:19:39.086 --> 00:19:39.590
as well--

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:43.064
developers working in
the community, something

00:19:43.064 --> 00:19:45.230
which you can go and run
on different infrastructure

00:19:45.230 --> 00:19:47.600
if you want to move,
that flexibility.

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:49.770
The other one is the Cloud ML.

00:19:49.770 --> 00:19:52.220
So here we're using
it to train our data.

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:54.759
And here we are
using it to predict.

00:19:54.759 --> 00:19:56.300
A question that
comes up quite often,

00:19:56.300 --> 00:19:57.380
if I create something
in TensorFlow,

00:19:57.380 --> 00:19:58.700
where am I going to
make my predictions?

00:19:58.700 --> 00:19:59.690
Well, it's up to you.

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:01.730
You could take your
TensorFlow model

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:04.590
and deploy a compiled
version to your mobile phone.

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:07.610
So you could, for example,
you would be doing Translate

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:09.994
and like, I've seen the example
where we actually take--

00:20:09.994 --> 00:20:11.660
you take your camera,
and you hold it up

00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:13.740
against a sign, for
example, in Japanese,

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:14.960
and then it gives
you the English text.

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:16.220
But let's say you're not
online at the moment.

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:18.510
You still want that
functionality to work.

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:20.310
So you might be saying
in your application,

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:22.101
if I don't have an
internet connection, use

00:20:22.101 --> 00:20:24.190
this local model, which
is going to be smaller.

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:25.640
It's going to be less powerful.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:28.130
If I do have a connection,
then use this model.

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:29.630
And then you would
make an API call,

00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:33.020
and then you can come back
to Cloud Machine Learning.

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:34.587
So you have that ability.

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:42.520
TensorFlow, itself, I'm letting
here into some of the things

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:45.610
I explain to developers
as well, because I

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:49.480
think this is where things
kind of go wrong if you're not

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:51.110
asking these questions.

00:20:51.110 --> 00:20:54.220
So I just told you about all
the good thing about TensorFlow.

00:20:54.220 --> 00:20:56.092
This is the engine
of TensorFlow.

00:20:56.092 --> 00:20:57.550
If you want improve
the engine, you

00:20:57.550 --> 00:21:00.220
have people work together
to actually collaborate

00:21:00.220 --> 00:21:00.872
in that space.

00:21:00.872 --> 00:21:02.830
But there's a lot going
on there at the moment.

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:04.020
It's pretty solid.

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:06.370
As you go up the
stack, these are

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:09.280
the kind of important things
to pay attention to, layers.

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:11.350
If you're building
models there, it's

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:13.940
a great place to learn
about machine learning.

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:15.700
But it's a place
that, when you want

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:17.960
to move from one
architecture to another,

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:20.490
you need to go and think about
how is that going to look?

00:21:20.490 --> 00:21:22.420
How's that process
going to go run?

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:25.480
That's why we created estimators
and canned estimators.

00:21:25.480 --> 00:21:28.610
An estimator itself gives you
the ability to actually just

00:21:28.610 --> 00:21:29.660
say, this is my model.

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:32.200
This is something to think
about, doing all the wiring up.

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:34.070
And we have a custom
estimator as well.

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:36.430
So you can take advantage
of something very specific

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:37.810
that you want to create.

00:21:37.810 --> 00:21:40.480
And you can take
that model and then

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:41.860
run it on your local machine.

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:44.164
But then also say, to
Cloud ML for example,

00:21:44.164 --> 00:21:46.330
go and run this in a cloud
and train it on big data.

00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:48.670
So it's really important--
the thing to take home

00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:50.350
is, really important
to challenge

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:53.140
people, at what level are they
interacting with this stack?

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:53.729
Where are we?

00:21:53.729 --> 00:21:54.520
Where should we be?

00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:56.728
And right at the top there
are the canned estimators.

00:21:56.728 --> 00:21:59.800
And these things these very,
these are out of the box.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:02.051
They're not like the
machine learning APIs

00:22:02.051 --> 00:22:02.800
that I showed you.

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:05.230
You still have to actually tell
how your data is going to fit,

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:06.400
and you have to
extract the features

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:07.994
and do more of
that kind of stuff.

00:22:07.994 --> 00:22:09.910
But basically, if you've
got a common problem,

00:22:09.910 --> 00:22:11.050
like a common
regression problem,

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:12.670
something like that,
there are estimators there

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:13.880
that you can use as well.

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:14.540
And this stuff, Keras.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:16.420
Keras is other framework
which you can use,

00:22:16.420 --> 00:22:17.462
which we support as well.

00:22:17.462 --> 00:22:19.503
A lot of data scientists
are using Keras as well.

00:22:19.503 --> 00:22:21.430
It also gives you
flexibility of back ends.

00:22:21.430 --> 00:22:23.206
So there are things
to think about there.

00:22:23.206 --> 00:22:24.580
And there are
things that to have

00:22:24.580 --> 00:22:27.632
that discussion with your lead
data scientist, or the product

00:22:27.632 --> 00:22:29.090
manager, or your
data science team,

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:30.930
and say, how we are
actually going to do this?

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:32.596
Or your data engineering
team-- and just

00:22:32.596 --> 00:22:34.300
start asking some
of these questions.

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:37.210
It's not about necessarily
actually understanding what's

00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:38.470
going on in all those layers.

00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:40.329
But its having
good answers like,

00:22:40.329 --> 00:22:41.620
are you going to build a model?

00:22:41.620 --> 00:22:43.416
OK, can we train that
in different spaces?

00:22:43.416 --> 00:22:45.040
What's the effort
that you need to make

00:22:45.040 --> 00:22:47.470
to help me get this to
production, because I want this

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:49.600
in production because I
want to actually start

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:54.160
offering new capabilities
to my customers.

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:56.030
This, if you get
any time left today,

00:22:56.030 --> 00:22:59.200
which I'm imagining you won't,
but if you do have any time,

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:01.900
downstairs is an
instant Insights demo.

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:05.730
And basically they show Cloud ML
making predictions about which

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.380
advert to show which taxi.

00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:10.649
So imagine taxis
driving around town.

00:23:10.649 --> 00:23:12.690
Some are coming from the
airport going into town.

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:13.773
Other ones are going back.

00:23:13.773 --> 00:23:15.630
Some have three people
and some have two.

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:17.800
And this is a running
model you can go and look

00:23:17.800 --> 00:23:19.840
at and basically
see the whole flow.

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:21.024
So we take the message.

00:23:21.024 --> 00:23:23.440
And once we've received that
message, we make a prediction

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:25.780
and we say, oh, look, there's
one person in the taxi.

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:26.680
They're heading to the airport.

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:28.110
Maybe they'd like
to have a coffee.

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:29.860
Or there are three
people going into town.

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:33.220
Maybe they'd like to go
to this theater show.

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:35.570
You can draw your own
conclusions what's a good idea.

00:23:35.570 --> 00:23:37.335
But the thing is, this
is all serverless.

00:23:37.335 --> 00:23:39.460
So you can actually go and
see how you would set up

00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:40.712
this production pipeline.

00:23:40.712 --> 00:23:42.170
And the other thing
is here as well

00:23:42.170 --> 00:23:45.280
to see is that we can take data
flow, and the same data that's

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:47.560
coming in to make our
predictions, we can filter

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:50.060
off and have it go
into our, for example,

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:51.880
bit query, which is
our data warehouse,

00:23:51.880 --> 00:23:54.640
streaming real time with the
data that' already there.

00:23:54.640 --> 00:23:56.849
So when someone phones up
and says there's a problem,

00:23:56.849 --> 00:23:59.140
you can actually look at the
data you've been receiving

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:01.660
and query not only the data you
had yesterday, but the stuff

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:03.640
that's coming in now.

00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:05.680
These kind of
capabilities enable

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:07.060
you to act in this space.

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:08.800
They enable you to
make things quickly.

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:11.380
They enable you to
get it to production.

00:24:11.380 --> 00:24:13.030
And they enable you
to actually start

00:24:13.030 --> 00:24:15.550
working with it in production
when you have problems.

00:24:15.550 --> 00:24:19.660
That's the story that we
have around machine learning.

