have [INAUDIBLE]??

00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:06.947
ROBERT SAXBY: So I would
say there's a few answers.

00:25:06.947 --> 00:25:07.780
So the question is--

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:08.120
AUDIENCE: Repeat the question.

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:10.328
ROBERT SAXBY: Yeah, I'm
going I'm going to repeat it.

00:25:10.328 --> 00:25:11.170
Yeah, I got that.

00:25:11.170 --> 00:25:14.180
So the question is, when
natural language processing,

00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:17.410
how do we cope with
different stacks?

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:19.250
Which one should we use?

00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:22.000
Is one open, the
other one closed?

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:25.270
How do we navigate that space?

00:25:25.270 --> 00:25:28.990
So the open APIs that
we have, those APIs

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:31.030
will give you capability
out of the box.

00:25:31.030 --> 00:25:34.060
And so if I was
designing an application

00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:36.250
and I wanted, for
example, my application

00:25:36.250 --> 00:25:38.630
is, let's say I'm taking--

00:25:38.630 --> 00:25:40.420
we used an example
earlier with that chat.

00:25:40.420 --> 00:25:43.090
So the chat's coming in and
I want to process a chat

00:25:43.090 --> 00:25:45.010
and actually have
a signal real time,

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:47.980
about saying if the person
is happy or angry so I can,

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:51.186
after the chat's finished, I can
maybe steer that chat somewhere

00:25:51.186 --> 00:25:52.810
else to actually have
someone call back

00:25:52.810 --> 00:25:54.430
or look into what
was going wrong,

00:25:54.430 --> 00:25:55.982
and give a very
short turnaround.

00:25:55.982 --> 00:25:57.940
If I was looking at
bringing those capabilities

00:25:57.940 --> 00:26:00.250
to an application,
let's say soon,

00:26:00.250 --> 00:26:01.709
quickly, I would
look at those APIs

00:26:01.709 --> 00:26:04.249
and see if that fits, because
that's something you can really

00:26:04.249 --> 00:26:05.639
speed up your development.

00:26:05.639 --> 00:26:07.930
The good thing there, and if
you're going in that space

00:26:07.930 --> 00:26:12.830
is, choose an API which is on
a good standard, a good rest

00:26:12.830 --> 00:26:13.510
standard.

00:26:13.510 --> 00:26:16.456
And you can actually, it's
a little work to implement.

00:26:16.456 --> 00:26:18.080
And you can actually,
let's say, proxy,

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:19.705
so you can put
something in front of it

00:26:19.705 --> 00:26:21.020
and actually work with it.

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:22.436
And if you want
to change that out

00:26:22.436 --> 00:26:24.890
for some other capability
or some other vendor later,

00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:27.460
the engineering
effort is very small.

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:29.511
If you're going to go,
let's say more specific,

00:26:29.511 --> 00:26:31.510
and you're really trying
to target nuances which

00:26:31.510 --> 00:26:35.620
are very unique to use case,
use something like TensorFlow

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:39.509
as opposed to, let's say,
one of our competitor's

00:26:39.509 --> 00:26:41.050
offerings, which is
a little bit more

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:43.660
closed, because TensorFlow
is an open project.

00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:46.990
If you get into problems,
it's not about one company

00:26:46.990 --> 00:26:48.060
saying this is our thing.

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:51.370
It's something supported
by a whole group of people.

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:54.070
And they will give you
help in that space.

00:26:54.070 --> 00:26:57.580
They will give you a chance to
take the development further.

00:26:57.580 --> 00:26:59.680
And importantly, a question
I got asked recently

00:26:59.680 --> 00:27:01.888
and I think it's really
worth thinking about as well,

00:27:01.888 --> 00:27:04.000
was at some point
some organizations are

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:05.330
going to get asked a question.

00:27:05.330 --> 00:27:08.350
And that question is going to
be, to an auditor, explain me

00:27:08.350 --> 00:27:10.890
how it works.

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:13.370
And that is going to
be an exciting time.

00:27:13.370 --> 00:27:15.490
So if you get that
question, it's

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:18.290
going to be really good to have
a system that you're using,

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:21.010
which is open and you can
use tools to actually look

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:23.860
at what's going in at
each level to provide data

00:27:23.860 --> 00:27:26.334
to the auditor saying look,
you might not understand it,

00:27:26.334 --> 00:27:28.000
but at least I can
show what's going on.

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:29.055
This is happening there.

00:27:29.055 --> 00:27:31.180
It's not just some black
box and your core business

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:33.340
is now tied into this thing
that you can just say,

00:27:33.340 --> 00:27:37.090
well, I ask it something
and it gives me an answer.

00:27:37.090 --> 00:27:38.800
There's a big dialogue
around TensorFlow.

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:40.716
And even the models that
we use internally, we

00:27:40.716 --> 00:27:41.920
have a lot of effort spent.

00:27:41.920 --> 00:27:44.650
For example, for an
inception model for images

00:27:44.650 --> 00:27:46.840
is a trained model in images
which we've brought out

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:49.376
into community so other people
can develop their own image.

00:27:49.376 --> 00:27:51.500
So there's a lot of shared
knowledge in this space.

00:27:51.500 --> 00:27:52.660
So I'd really tried
to be in that space

00:27:52.660 --> 00:27:55.390
with the shared knowledge, so
you can have access to people

00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:58.660
but also get access to what's
going on when you need to.

00:27:58.660 --> 00:28:00.460
Does that answer your question?

