00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:10.230
use in their mobile device.

00:31:10.230 --> 00:31:11.790
And at Google, we
see the same sort

00:31:11.790 --> 00:31:13.620
of transformation going on.

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:17.620
More than 50% of all the
queries happen on this device.

00:31:17.620 --> 00:31:22.260
But what really strikes me
is that 20% of these queries

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:24.780
are voice based.

00:31:24.780 --> 00:31:29.160
And that leads me to
the following thought.

00:31:29.160 --> 00:31:33.300
This is a new area of
consumer experience.

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:35.370
It creates a new standard.

00:31:35.370 --> 00:31:37.950
So think of it as,
you're not only

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:40.290
competing against
other companies,

00:31:40.290 --> 00:31:42.810
but you're actually
competing against the best

00:31:42.810 --> 00:31:48.760
experience a consumer has ever
had, regardless of industry.

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:51.112
So at Google, we
have the mission

00:31:51.112 --> 00:31:52.570
to organize the
world's information

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:55.870
and make it relevant and
universally accessible.

00:31:55.870 --> 00:31:58.620
And we do that in
many ways and shapes.

00:31:58.620 --> 00:32:00.720
So what we're
going to do next is

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:03.750
to give you a closer look how
we are using that in the Google

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:08.550
Home, on your mobile
device, and even in the car.

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:11.250
And more importantly,
how you as a business

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:13.270
can also benefit from that.

00:32:13.270 --> 00:32:16.380
So with that I'd like to
introduce my colleague, Lee.

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:17.760
Give her a big
round of applause.

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:19.260
And after that I'll
come back to you

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:21.594
to explain a little bit
more about the challenge.

00:32:27.947 --> 00:32:29.030
LEE VONSTRA: Hi, everyone.

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:30.340
My name is Lee Vonstra.

00:32:30.340 --> 00:32:32.470
I work as a customer
engineer for Google,

00:32:32.470 --> 00:32:34.060
for Google Cloud Team.

00:32:34.060 --> 00:32:35.890
And I'm here today
to talk a little bit

00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:38.020
about Google Home
and Google Assistant,

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:40.550
just how that works
all under the hood--

00:32:40.550 --> 00:32:42.790
also chatbots.

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:45.820
This picture that you see
here is actually a picture

00:32:45.820 --> 00:32:48.910
that was made in 2016 of
what's on the Google I/O.

00:32:48.910 --> 00:32:50.720
This is our CEO, Sundar.

00:32:50.720 --> 00:32:54.430
And he came on stage and he
said like, at this time from now

00:32:54.430 --> 00:32:58.930
on, we will move from a mobile
first world to a more AI

00:32:58.930 --> 00:33:00.490
first world.

00:33:00.490 --> 00:33:02.440
Now what does he mean?

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:05.560
Back in the past when we
were creating applications,

00:33:05.560 --> 00:33:08.224
we made it that way,
that we'll make sure

00:33:08.224 --> 00:33:10.390
that it works on a mobile
phone, because if it works

00:33:10.390 --> 00:33:13.140
on a mobile phone then
we can use resize it,

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:14.950
do responsive design
and so that it

00:33:14.950 --> 00:33:16.930
runs across any type of device.

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:20.560
And for a very long time,
that was our way of thinking,

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:24.160
our design thinking how we
would create applications.

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:27.730
But now we are on a time that
we collected so much data

00:33:27.730 --> 00:33:30.880
and we have so much
computing power in the cloud

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:33.730
that we finally can
change our way of thinking

00:33:33.730 --> 00:33:36.700
and make a more AI
first approach, where

00:33:36.700 --> 00:33:40.420
we let our applications
or interfaces be

00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:43.540
created by machine learning.

00:33:43.540 --> 00:33:45.730
And we do that with
the Google Assistant.

00:33:45.730 --> 00:33:50.110
And the Google Assistant,
that is our smart assistant,

00:33:50.110 --> 00:33:54.370
is implemented in
all the latest Google

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:57.650
products that you can buy, like
think about the Google Watch,

00:33:57.650 --> 00:33:59.640
think about the Android phone.

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:03.370
Think all Android phones
from the Marshmallow on up.

00:34:03.370 --> 00:34:06.430
The Google Home, the smart
speaker, but also in cars

00:34:06.430 --> 00:34:08.996
we're try to implement
there so that you can,

00:34:08.996 --> 00:34:10.370
when you're driving
in a car, you

00:34:10.370 --> 00:34:14.830
can ask like, oh, how
is my commute to Paris?

00:34:14.830 --> 00:34:16.174
How should I drive?

00:34:16.174 --> 00:34:18.880
Can you turn on
the radio, instead

00:34:18.880 --> 00:34:23.150
of looking through a screen
or interacting with a screen.

00:34:23.150 --> 00:34:24.790
So it's a conversation
between you

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:29.500
and Google that helps you to get
more things done in this world.

00:34:29.500 --> 00:34:31.690
Now just to make a
couple of things clear,

00:34:31.690 --> 00:34:35.287
the Google Home, that is the
voice activated speaker and it

00:34:35.287 --> 00:34:37.120
really is nothing more
than a speaker that's

00:34:37.120 --> 00:34:38.770
connected to the internet.

00:34:38.770 --> 00:34:41.130
As a microphone,
you can talk to it,

00:34:41.130 --> 00:34:43.989
but the whole magic around
it, that is actually

00:34:43.989 --> 00:34:45.489
to Google Assistant.

00:34:45.489 --> 00:34:48.969
So that is the conversation
between Google and you,

00:34:48.969 --> 00:34:50.570
and that is implemented
in the phones,

00:34:50.570 --> 00:34:53.320
but also in the Google Home.

00:34:53.320 --> 00:34:55.630
Now you will also
hear me a couple times

00:34:55.630 --> 00:34:58.640
referring to actions on Google.

00:34:58.640 --> 00:34:59.890
What is that?

00:34:59.890 --> 00:35:02.380
Think about it as apps.

00:35:02.380 --> 00:35:05.860
It's how you can
extend the system.

00:35:05.860 --> 00:35:10.870
So you can create your own
conversations, your own chat

00:35:10.870 --> 00:35:15.190
logics, and implement that on
top of the Google Assistant.

00:35:15.190 --> 00:35:18.020
And we can do that
with tools like API.AI,

00:35:18.020 --> 00:35:21.130
which I will talk about later.

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:23.380
But just to let you know
what Google Assistant can do,

00:35:23.380 --> 00:35:26.240
you can ask the Google
Assistant to manage tasks.

00:35:26.240 --> 00:35:29.680
This photo that you
see here is actually

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:32.170
a question to the
assistant, like, yeah,

00:35:32.170 --> 00:35:36.820
what's my confirmation number
to my flight to Philadelphia?

00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:40.446
And then the Google Assistant
will answer you with the PNR

00:35:40.446 --> 00:35:43.120
that they got from the flight.

00:35:43.120 --> 00:35:44.830
How does the
assistant know that?

00:35:44.830 --> 00:35:47.050
Well, you're locked into
a Google account, which

00:35:47.050 --> 00:35:49.050
has connection to your
email and all that,

00:35:49.050 --> 00:35:50.570
and your calendar and so on.

00:35:50.570 --> 00:35:52.960
So the Google Assistant
is smart enough

00:35:52.960 --> 00:35:55.330
to figure that out for you.

00:35:55.330 --> 00:36:00.760
You can ask things like, OK,
how will how will my transit be?

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:01.865
Is it busy on the road?

00:36:01.865 --> 00:36:03.490
And the Google
Assistant will know that

00:36:03.490 --> 00:36:06.460
by connecting to all kinds
of external services.

00:36:06.460 --> 00:36:08.420
So you can use it
to plan the day.

00:36:08.420 --> 00:36:10.310
But you can also use
it to ask questions.

00:36:10.310 --> 00:36:12.490
So I could, for
example, ask like,

00:36:12.490 --> 00:36:14.770
who's the King of
the Netherlands?

00:36:14.770 --> 00:36:17.890
And the assistant will answer,
well, that's Willem-Alexander.

00:36:17.890 --> 00:36:20.360
But then my next question
could be like, OK,

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:22.540
and what's the name of his wife?

00:36:22.540 --> 00:36:24.790
And then, the
Assistant will know

00:36:24.790 --> 00:36:28.640
that the context is
Willem-Alexander, so his wife,

00:36:28.640 --> 00:36:31.480
her name is Maxima.

00:36:31.480 --> 00:36:34.450
So I mean that is something
that is very smart.

00:36:34.450 --> 00:36:39.442
You need more than a decision
tree to figure that out.

00:36:39.442 --> 00:36:40.900
You need to understand
the context.

00:36:40.900 --> 00:36:42.670
You need to understand
what was passed in

00:36:42.670 --> 00:36:44.980
and to keep that context.

00:36:44.980 --> 00:36:47.110
You can use also the
Assistant for making memory,

00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:50.260
so I can ask, like, what is my
favorite-- my favorite color is

00:36:50.260 --> 00:36:50.769
blue.

00:36:50.769 --> 00:36:52.810
And if I ask the Google
Assistant next week, what

00:36:52.810 --> 00:36:54.790
is my favorite color again?

00:36:54.790 --> 00:36:58.360
Then the Assistant will know,
well, you told me it was blue.

00:36:58.360 --> 00:36:59.960
So it can remember things.

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:02.560
And you can also use it
to control your home,

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:06.150
if you have all types of smart
devices like the Philips hue

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:07.445
bulbs or the Nest.

00:37:07.445 --> 00:37:10.000
You can say, oh,
turn on the lights.

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:11.870
Turn up the heat.

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:16.390
Turn the volume on, or
play my favorite TV show

00:37:16.390 --> 00:37:18.677
from YouTube or on Netflix.

00:37:18.677 --> 00:37:20.260
As long as you have
smart devices that

00:37:20.260 --> 00:37:22.290
are connected to
the internet, then

00:37:22.290 --> 00:37:24.980
your Google Assistant can
communicate with that.

00:37:28.580 --> 00:37:31.950
Here, I came up
with an example how

00:37:31.950 --> 00:37:36.480
you can implement that logic for
Google Home, or for the Google

00:37:36.480 --> 00:37:39.010
Assistant, within your company.

00:37:39.010 --> 00:37:41.010
I mean, we're working
with companies,

00:37:41.010 --> 00:37:45.670
and I assume that we all have
internal applications running.

00:37:45.670 --> 00:37:48.480
Those are applications that
are run behind a firewall.

00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:52.050
So one of such applications
could be a meeting room

00:37:52.050 --> 00:37:54.902
application, to
book a meeting room.

00:37:54.902 --> 00:37:58.650
So I could, for example,
ask the Assistant hey,

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:01.910
OK, Google, let me talk
the book a meeting room.

00:38:01.910 --> 00:38:04.830
Book a meeting room in this
case is my custom action.

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:08.494
It is the action that I, as
a developer, will create.

00:38:08.494 --> 00:38:10.410
And what you will see
is, the Google Assistant

00:38:10.410 --> 00:38:11.130
will talk back.

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:14.400
And it will say like, OK, sure
here is book a meeting room.

00:38:14.400 --> 00:38:17.100
And then, at that moment,
the voice of the assistants

00:38:17.100 --> 00:38:20.400
will change, because then
it will go to a voice

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:23.390
that the developer chose
for their custom action.

00:38:23.390 --> 00:38:26.490
So you're coming into the
custom action flow now.

00:38:26.490 --> 00:38:29.820
So think about it
as an app or a flow.

00:38:29.820 --> 00:38:32.130
And it will start asking
with the welcome in text,

00:38:32.130 --> 00:38:33.100
like, OK, welcome.

00:38:33.100 --> 00:38:34.690
How can I help you?

00:38:34.690 --> 00:38:37.170
And I say like, OK, yeah,
I'd like to book a meeting

00:38:37.170 --> 00:38:40.010
room for three persons.

00:38:40.010 --> 00:38:41.850
Sure, for when?

00:38:41.850 --> 00:38:44.630
Tomorrow from 2:00 to 3:00 PM.

00:38:44.630 --> 00:38:48.420
And what happens here is,
tomorrow from 2:00 to 3:00 PM--

00:38:48.420 --> 00:38:50.890
or I want to book a meeting
room for three persons.

00:38:50.890 --> 00:38:53.280
Three persons is a
parameter that will

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:55.084
be sent to an external system.

00:38:55.084 --> 00:38:57.000
So imagine that you have
a book a meeting room

00:38:57.000 --> 00:38:59.370
internal application,
which has a REST API.

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:03.600
I just pass in three
persons, and then the API

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:04.500
would handle it back.

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:08.160
It would say, OK, the
next request that I should

00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:10.560
make is a request for the time.

00:39:10.560 --> 00:39:13.110
OK, tomorrow from
2:00 to 3:00 PM.

00:39:13.110 --> 00:39:16.640
And then the system will tell
me back like, OK, it went fine.

00:39:16.640 --> 00:39:18.030
The meeting room is booked.

00:39:18.030 --> 00:39:20.700
And then the Assistant
can tell me that.

00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:23.610
Or, the meeting room was
not booked because maybe it

00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:24.780
was already booked before.

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:29.770
And then it can come up
with a second suggestion.

00:39:29.770 --> 00:39:33.600
Something like this is
not so difficult to create

00:39:33.600 --> 00:39:36.350
with a tool like API.AI.

00:39:36.350 --> 00:39:39.010
API.AI is like a
developer console.

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:41.980
It's like a SaaS application,
a website you call there.

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:44.070
And you can create
all these intents,

00:39:44.070 --> 00:39:47.210
so you can write out
these conversations.

00:39:47.210 --> 00:39:49.510
But it also contains
machine learning

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:53.180
so you can train the system.

00:39:53.180 --> 00:39:56.380
So when you ask questions,
it becomes smarter over time.

00:39:56.380 --> 00:39:58.700
If you ask the question
a little bit wrong,

00:39:58.700 --> 00:40:00.190
it will understand
which question

00:40:00.190 --> 00:40:04.186
you meant because it got trained
through machine learning.

00:40:04.186 --> 00:40:08.540
So API.AI is a developer console
that you can start using.

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:11.710
And when you use that, you
create your conversation

00:40:11.710 --> 00:40:12.910
only once.

00:40:12.910 --> 00:40:15.970
And then you can implement
it for various solutions.

00:40:15.970 --> 00:40:19.480
So I can deploy
it on Google Home.

00:40:19.480 --> 00:40:21.490
I can deploy it on
Facebook as a bot,

00:40:21.490 --> 00:40:24.270
and then I need to add
that person, the bot,

00:40:24.270 --> 00:40:26.320
as a friend in my
Facebook account

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:28.220
and I can start talking to it.

00:40:28.220 --> 00:40:30.160
I can have a chatbot
on my website.

00:40:30.160 --> 00:40:32.980
I can implement it
easily with an I-frame,

00:40:32.980 --> 00:40:37.300
or I can integrate it real in
my own system and in my own look

00:40:37.300 --> 00:40:39.280
and feel for phones.

00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:41.260
You name it, we have
all the integrations

00:40:41.260 --> 00:40:45.250
already available in
API.AI developer console.

00:40:45.250 --> 00:40:48.780
It's just a matter
of flipping a switch.

00:40:48.780 --> 00:40:52.450
Actions SDK, that is a
client library that's

00:40:52.450 --> 00:40:55.060
currently written in note.

00:40:55.060 --> 00:40:59.690
That is in-case API.AI
is limited for you,

00:40:59.690 --> 00:41:03.350
or in case you want to extend
and do some more magic,

00:41:03.350 --> 00:41:07.985
then you can choose to implement
all these ideas by yourself.

00:41:07.985 --> 00:41:10.540
It is a little bit more work,
because then you also need

00:41:10.540 --> 00:41:13.390
to come up with natural
language processing and machine

00:41:13.390 --> 00:41:18.901
learning, which API.AI
has out of the box.

00:41:18.901 --> 00:41:20.650
So here you can see
it again, that can you

00:41:20.650 --> 00:41:24.000
book a meeting room for
tomorrow from 2:00 to 3:00 PM.

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:25.990
I call that service
Book A Meeting Room,

00:41:25.990 --> 00:41:28.678
and I pass in tomorrow,
2:00 to 3:00 PM.

00:41:31.490 --> 00:41:36.620
The web intent, this is API.AI,
you create a web intent there.

00:41:36.620 --> 00:41:39.230
And you can see that
as a kind of flow,

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:41.480
it's a mapping between
what the user says

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:43.850
and the action that
needs to be taken.

00:41:43.850 --> 00:41:47.090
So for example, I created
a while ago a Google Home

00:41:47.090 --> 00:41:49.490
application where I could
ask to Google Home, hey,

00:41:49.490 --> 00:41:51.120
what's on TV tonight?

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:52.340
Oh, on what channel?

00:41:52.340 --> 00:41:55.030
I want to know what's
on TV on RTL 4.

00:41:55.030 --> 00:41:59.180
And then it would read out
a whole programming type.

00:41:59.180 --> 00:42:00.390
How would I do that?

00:42:00.390 --> 00:42:02.450
I would create a
web intent where

00:42:02.450 --> 00:42:06.140
I ask, what's on a channel?

00:42:06.140 --> 00:42:09.950
And then the user can pass in
whatever channel they like.

00:42:09.950 --> 00:42:13.320
But it will take that
whatever, this part,

00:42:13.320 --> 00:42:16.160
it will be fired
to the background.

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:18.680
And I assume that I
have some kind of magic,

00:42:18.680 --> 00:42:24.210
or some kind of service there,
that can query the TV Guide.

00:42:24.210 --> 00:42:26.730
But the thing that I passed
in, that is an entity.

00:42:26.730 --> 00:42:30.470
So you can pre-define
a bunch of entities.

00:42:30.470 --> 00:42:32.714
See it as parameters.

00:42:32.714 --> 00:42:34.880
So you know that there is
some kind of hard variable

00:42:34.880 --> 00:42:37.580
that you need to send to the
system so that you can act on.

00:42:41.772 --> 00:42:42.980
So this is what you see here.

00:42:42.980 --> 00:42:44.355
Here, you create
the web intents.

00:42:44.355 --> 00:42:45.510
They are the entities.

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:47.500
There is a section
for integrations.

00:42:47.500 --> 00:42:51.340
This is for changing if you
want and deploy it on Facebook,

00:42:51.340 --> 00:42:52.900
or on Slack, or on Skype.

00:42:52.900 --> 00:42:56.240
And there's also a section
for training the APIs.

00:42:56.240 --> 00:42:58.180
So if you have created
these web intents

00:42:58.180 --> 00:43:01.150
and people have been asking
questions, you can train it.

00:43:01.150 --> 00:43:04.152
You can correct it if it
gave you the wrong answer.

00:43:04.152 --> 00:43:06.610
You can say, OK, well, next
time don't answer this anymore,

00:43:06.610 --> 00:43:07.901
and then it will remember that.

00:43:10.750 --> 00:43:13.010
Here you see the
webhook in parameters.

00:43:13.010 --> 00:43:16.640
There So you can
create webhooks.

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:19.370
This is actually,
you enable a webhook

00:43:19.370 --> 00:43:23.570
and then you pass in the URL to
your external web applications.

00:43:23.570 --> 00:43:26.390
And then when you
create your intent,

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:28.910
you just click on Actions
and you say like, OK,

00:43:28.910 --> 00:43:32.450
every time when
I ask this event,

00:43:32.450 --> 00:43:34.790
or this intent,
actually you need

00:43:34.790 --> 00:43:37.430
to act on through a webhook.

00:43:37.430 --> 00:43:39.560
And then I give it a
webhook name, in this case,

00:43:39.560 --> 00:43:42.400
Get By Channel, that will
be passed to that URL.

00:43:46.220 --> 00:43:50.090
Now what I did in the
background for this is I

00:43:50.090 --> 00:43:51.510
used Cloud Functions.

00:43:51.510 --> 00:43:53.510
But you can use any type
of background you like.

00:43:53.510 --> 00:43:55.640
The reason why I choose
Google Cloud Functions

00:43:55.640 --> 00:43:57.470
is because, in
this case, I don't

00:43:57.470 --> 00:44:00.230
need to worry about the
virtual machine or server.

00:44:00.230 --> 00:44:03.350
I just create a function
somewhere in the cloud

00:44:03.350 --> 00:44:06.530
and then let Google scale
all the machines for me,

00:44:06.530 --> 00:44:08.850
and I just don't care
about backgrounds at all.

00:44:08.850 --> 00:44:10.850
So I just create a
JavaScript function

00:44:10.850 --> 00:44:13.680
in this case that would
query the TV Guide.

00:44:17.420 --> 00:44:19.170
I don't think we need
to go into this now.

00:44:19.170 --> 00:44:20.820
This is very technical.

00:44:20.820 --> 00:44:23.660
But ask me if you have
questions about it.

00:44:23.660 --> 00:44:25.830
Just so you know, these
kind of Cloud Functions,

00:44:25.830 --> 00:44:29.520
you can write that
on the Google Cloud

00:44:29.520 --> 00:44:32.356
in a native editor on
the Cloud platform.

00:44:32.356 --> 00:44:33.980
So you don't need to
install any tools.

00:44:33.980 --> 00:44:37.470
You can just do it right away.

00:44:37.470 --> 00:44:40.230
Yes, and then you
deploy your action.

00:44:40.230 --> 00:44:44.520
And once you deploy your
action, then you can test it.

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:47.040
So this is within the
Google Cloud Platform,

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:48.930
there is a web simulator.

00:44:48.930 --> 00:44:52.950
And there you can authorize
it with your Google account,

00:44:52.950 --> 00:44:57.000
and then you say like,
this is my API.AI account

00:44:57.000 --> 00:44:58.630
that I'm currently using.

00:44:58.630 --> 00:45:00.870
And at that moment,
in the simulator

00:45:00.870 --> 00:45:04.560
you get like a similar
behavior as if you

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:06.300
would have a real Google Home.

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:10.120
So I could start typing to it
and it gives me answers back.

00:45:10.120 --> 00:45:12.690
But I could also use the
microphone for my computer

00:45:12.690 --> 00:45:13.950
and ask questions.

00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:16.140
And then it would
read out the answers

00:45:16.140 --> 00:45:18.582
as if it would be
the Google Assistant.

00:45:21.990 --> 00:45:23.550
Once you're done,
when you're really

00:45:23.550 --> 00:45:28.370
happy with this conversation,
then you can deploy it.

00:45:28.370 --> 00:45:32.700
And kind of like an app store,
you need to register the name

00:45:32.700 --> 00:45:37.260
and then it will be whitelisted
in the Google App Store,

00:45:37.260 --> 00:45:40.275
or in Assistant
App Store, kind of.

00:45:40.275 --> 00:45:42.760
That means that there's
approval process.

00:45:42.760 --> 00:45:45.900
Somebody within Google
will check your bot,

00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:49.140
see if it's valid enough.

00:45:49.140 --> 00:45:52.090
See also if you don't let
the microphone open nonstop,

00:45:52.090 --> 00:45:54.270
because that is actually
one of the guidelines.

00:45:54.270 --> 00:45:56.710
You can never leave
the microphone open.

00:45:56.710 --> 00:45:59.280
Out of the box that doesn't
work on a Google Home.

00:45:59.280 --> 00:46:02.490
It always keeps it open
for a minute or maybe less,

00:46:02.490 --> 00:46:04.500
and then automatically
shuts off.

00:46:04.500 --> 00:46:06.180
But if you don't
let your users know

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:09.950
that the microphone is open,
then your [INAUDIBLE] process

00:46:09.950 --> 00:46:10.800
will fail.

00:46:10.800 --> 00:46:13.750
So make sure that you always
ask for more questions.

00:46:13.750 --> 00:46:16.400
So when you create
a conversation,

00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:19.360
you should always answer
like, OK, this is the answer.

00:46:19.360 --> 00:46:20.970
Do you like to hear more?

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:22.420
OK, and this is the answer.

00:46:25.480 --> 00:46:28.410
And in case you're really
interested in this,

00:46:28.410 --> 00:46:33.490
in creating Google Assistant
conversations, or using API.AI,

00:46:33.490 --> 00:46:36.010
there is this design
document that Google

00:46:36.010 --> 00:46:39.310
created that can help you
with creating conversations

00:46:39.310 --> 00:46:41.080
like this.

00:46:41.080 --> 00:46:43.480
Definitely a good read.

00:46:43.480 --> 00:46:47.190
I think with that I can
hand it over to you, Mario.

00:46:47.190 --> 00:46:48.140
MARIO: Thanks, Lee!

00:46:48.140 --> 00:46:48.848
LEE VONSTRA: Yes.

00:46:52.774 --> 00:46:53.940
MARIO: Well, thank you, Lee.

00:46:53.940 --> 00:46:56.910
What we try to do is
to basically show you

00:46:56.910 --> 00:46:58.740
the technical toolkit.

00:46:58.740 --> 00:47:00.990
So the next ingredient we
want to share with you,

00:47:00.990 --> 00:47:03.930
and after that you're really
going to get your hands dirty,

00:47:03.930 --> 00:47:06.390
is a conceptual
creative toolkit.

00:47:06.390 --> 00:47:11.650
So if you can switch to
the next slide, thank you.

00:47:11.650 --> 00:47:14.100
So this is all about
creating the future.

00:47:14.100 --> 00:47:15.810
You now have the
opportunity together

00:47:15.810 --> 00:47:18.030
to create a future together.

00:47:18.030 --> 00:47:20.370
If you go to next slide.

00:47:20.370 --> 00:47:25.140
So at Google, we think of
creativity and innovation

00:47:25.140 --> 00:47:26.850
not as an event.

00:47:26.850 --> 00:47:31.500
We see it as a process,
perhaps even more as a mindset.

00:47:31.500 --> 00:47:35.040
And we worked a
couple of years ago

00:47:35.040 --> 00:47:38.790
to get with Stanford's IDEO
and a couple of other experts

00:47:38.790 --> 00:47:43.050
to develop this method, which
is called designed thinking.

00:47:43.050 --> 00:47:45.552
And basically, it consists
of three main concepts.

00:47:45.552 --> 00:47:47.760
So I wanted to walk you
through, because I think it's

00:47:47.760 --> 00:47:50.170
very useful in your exercise.

00:47:50.170 --> 00:47:52.260
So the first one is,
if you are starting

00:47:52.260 --> 00:47:55.350
to build something you don't
start with a revenue model

00:47:55.350 --> 00:47:58.020
and buying some
great technology.

00:47:58.020 --> 00:47:59.962
It always starts with
knowing the user.

00:48:03.270 --> 00:48:06.720
Once you know the user,
their beliefs, their needs,

00:48:06.720 --> 00:48:08.350
that's the perfect
starting point

00:48:08.350 --> 00:48:10.920
to build a magical solution.

00:48:10.920 --> 00:48:12.600
Now the next part
you want to do--

00:48:12.600 --> 00:48:16.710
and basically, again
some practical guides.

00:48:16.710 --> 00:48:19.170
You could apply a
framework of three pillars

00:48:19.170 --> 00:48:20.250
of knowing the user.

00:48:20.250 --> 00:48:21.930
The first is analyze.

00:48:21.930 --> 00:48:25.650
So what data do you have
available to analyze the user?

00:48:25.650 --> 00:48:27.930
Second one is observe.

00:48:27.930 --> 00:48:30.720
There are ways you can
observe your users.

00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:33.990
And the third one
is to ask them.

00:48:33.990 --> 00:48:38.100
There's no better inputs than
asking a user how they feel,

00:48:38.100 --> 00:48:39.960
what they need, et cetera.

00:48:39.960 --> 00:48:43.920
So the second one, Andrea
Cabus already touched upon.

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:47.520
We don't think in incremental
improvements of 10%,

00:48:47.520 --> 00:48:51.240
but we want to improve
and think about 10x.

00:48:51.240 --> 00:48:55.380
A perfect Google example,
I think, is Google Loon.

00:48:55.380 --> 00:48:58.470
In rural areas where
there was no Wi-Fi,

00:48:58.470 --> 00:49:01.440
it was a childish idea to
come up with air balloons that

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:04.320
could send out Wi-Fi signals.

00:49:04.320 --> 00:49:08.400
But it's these ideas that
in a grown up setting

00:49:08.400 --> 00:49:13.020
are considered impossible,
which are the strongest ideas.

00:49:13.020 --> 00:49:15.630
So ask yourself in
the team, what if,

00:49:15.630 --> 00:49:19.090
because asking the what if
question will make it bigger.

00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:22.140
And a final one is
be proto driven.

00:49:22.140 --> 00:49:25.650
You want to make sure
that you fail fast,

00:49:25.650 --> 00:49:29.340
because if you
incorporate that, you

00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:32.550
will learn if you succeed,
but also if you fail you

00:49:32.550 --> 00:49:34.830
can incorporate that learning.

00:49:34.830 --> 00:49:39.870
So we see the success not by
how high your success rate is,

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:41.973
but how often you
have tried something.

00:49:52.600 --> 00:49:53.650
OK, go to the next one.

00:49:57.840 --> 00:50:03.230
All right, so coming back, Lee
gave you the technical toolkit,

00:50:03.230 --> 00:50:06.290
how you can build a
conversational customer

00:50:06.290 --> 00:50:07.670
experience.

00:50:07.670 --> 00:50:11.630
I hope I've just gave you
a little bit of a toolkit

00:50:11.630 --> 00:50:15.230
how you can have a creative
process to come up.

00:50:15.230 --> 00:50:16.830
So we're going to
put that together.

00:50:16.830 --> 00:50:20.240
So the challenge,
we ask you, and you

00:50:20.240 --> 00:50:24.640
can continue the conversation
you had in your roundtable,

00:50:24.640 --> 00:50:29.540
redesign the customer experience
by creating a concept that

00:50:29.540 --> 00:50:31.640
uses this conversational AI.

00:50:31.640 --> 00:50:34.550
So that's your objective.

00:50:34.550 --> 00:50:36.080
We're going to have
hand out tables

00:50:36.080 --> 00:50:38.540
on the round tables,
which is a framework you

00:50:38.540 --> 00:50:41.810
can use to put in your ideas.

00:50:41.810 --> 00:50:44.180
You're going to be time limited.

00:50:44.180 --> 00:50:45.830
I'm sorry, we're
already far over time.

00:50:45.830 --> 00:50:50.510
But again, you only
have 20 minutes.

00:50:50.510 --> 00:50:52.400
Your chance to
win a Google Home,

00:50:52.400 --> 00:50:55.250
I'm afraid I can't keep
up with that promise.

00:50:57.790 --> 00:50:59.540
We don't have Google
Homes, unfortunately.

00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:00.740
But the important thing is--

00:51:03.780 --> 00:51:05.360
it was not intended
to be in there.

00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:06.740
So apology.

00:51:09.250 --> 00:51:12.140
It's going to be
Netherlands, end of year.

00:51:12.140 --> 00:51:14.829
We're going to score
based on business impact.

00:51:14.829 --> 00:51:16.370
So how big is the
impact you're going

00:51:16.370 --> 00:51:18.440
to come up with the concept?

00:51:18.440 --> 00:51:21.140
What's the customer
experience like?

00:51:21.140 --> 00:51:23.359
So who is your customer?

00:51:23.359 --> 00:51:25.025
What are the integrations
you could use?

00:51:25.025 --> 00:51:29.150
So that could be your own
data, could be external data.

00:51:29.150 --> 00:51:31.130
And finally,
originality-- could you

00:51:31.130 --> 00:51:34.110
make a flow describing
the conversation

00:51:34.110 --> 00:51:36.260
you would be having
with a customer,

00:51:36.260 --> 00:51:39.060
with one of your partners,
employees, et cetera?

00:51:41.720 --> 00:51:42.820
OK, go to the next slide.

00:51:45.330 --> 00:51:49.820
So as a final example, to give
you some food for thought,

00:51:49.820 --> 00:51:52.700
this is a very cool concept that
eBay demoed a couple of weeks

00:51:52.700 --> 00:51:53.850
ago.

00:51:53.850 --> 00:51:56.470
So I bought a camera
six years ago,

00:51:56.470 --> 00:52:00.020
but with the technological
advancement of smartphones

00:52:00.020 --> 00:52:04.070
I think the camera in my pocket
is far more powerful and easy

00:52:04.070 --> 00:52:05.040
to use.

00:52:05.040 --> 00:52:08.730
So I don't use my
bit camera anymore.

00:52:08.730 --> 00:52:10.760
So what I actually
could ask Ebay is,

00:52:10.760 --> 00:52:12.772
how much is my camera worth?

00:52:12.772 --> 00:52:14.480
And it would ask me
a couple of questions

00:52:14.480 --> 00:52:17.960
like, OK, tell me the brand,
the color, the condition.

00:52:17.960 --> 00:52:20.390
And it would then search
in its own great database

00:52:20.390 --> 00:52:23.270
and could provide me with
a very precise estimation

00:52:23.270 --> 00:52:25.820
of how much my camera is worth.

00:52:25.820 --> 00:52:28.010
I think these are
the kinds of concepts

00:52:28.010 --> 00:52:29.640
you might be thinking about.

00:52:29.640 --> 00:52:32.090
So with that, I wish
you all good luck.

00:52:32.090 --> 00:52:35.147
And the 20 minutes starts now.

